The CLI-vs-TUI call
Ship the CLI now. Build the TUI later, in this same repo — not a separate project.

Why one repo: the whole architecture is one chokepoint, one Store, one Net. A second codebase would fork the security-critical parts and you'd have to re-learn every proxy-bypass lesson (no_proxy, non-literal http://, reused daemons) a second time.
Why the CLI is close: the session prompt already is a mini interactive player — search/likes/playlist, play N, next/prev, shuffle/repeat, auto-advance announcements, np/vol/toggle/audit/rotate, nothing touching argv or shell history. What's missing is a short, contained list (below), none of it in pkg/net.
Is the session prompt's code redundant with a future TUI? No — complementary. It already de-risked the hard parts (holding the bridge open, the queue-as-cursor model, track-change detection, egress-ledger data for a status bar). What it can't do yet: non-blocking resolves, scrollable long lists, an event-driven IPC client. The recommended move — pull listQueue out of package main into pkg/ as a model that returns values/events instead of printing — fixes the worst CLI bug and becomes the TUI's foundation at the same time.
